<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Barbados</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Barbados is an island nation in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-ocean">Atlantic Ocean</a>, the southernmost and easternmost island of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> region. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinago">Kalinago</a> people lived there from the 13th century, but the indigenous population had mostly been sold into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, killed or died of disease by the time an English ship arrived on 14 May 1625. It became an English and later British colony with a plantation economy that used Irish indentured servants and then enslaved Africans. Barbados became independent on 30 November 1966 and a republic within the Commonwealth on 30 November 2021.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/barbados.jpg" alt="Barbados" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Denelson83 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Barbados.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Overseas commerce grew rapidly under the Navigation Acts, which reserved colonial trade for British ships: sugar from Jamaica and <b>Barbados</b>, tobacco from Virginia and Maryland and textiles from India were re-exported across Europe, and Glasgow prospered on tobacco after the union.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The United States built the canal between 1904 and 1914, after William Gorgas’s sanitation campaign brought yellow fever and malaria under control, employing tens of thousands of workers, many of them from <b>Barbados</b>, Jamaica and other Caribbean islands, whose descendants form an important Afro-Caribbean, English-speaking community.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-panama">Republic of Panama · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The first lasting colony on the Suriname River was founded in 1650 by settlers sent from <b>Barbados</b> by Francis Willoughby, Lord Willoughby of Parham, and by the 1660s this English colony, Willoughbyland, had several dozen sugar plantations worked by enslaved Africans.</blockquote><a href="/empire/suriname">Suriname · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Rastas have campaigned for the legalisation of cannabis: Jamaica decriminalised personal possession of up to two ounces in 2015, and <b>Barbados</b> legalised its use in religious settings in 2019.</blockquote><a href="/religion/rastafari">Rastafari · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>At the time of Spanish contact the Kalinago were among the dominant peoples of the Caribbean, living throughout the Windward Islands, Dominica and the southern Leeward Islands, including Guadeloupe, and possibly <b>Barbados</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/kalinago">Kalinago · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>In the British Caribbean the enslaved rose in <b>Barbados</b> in 1816 and Demerara in 1823, and at Christmas 1831 tens of thousands in western Jamaica followed the Baptist deacon Samuel Sharpe in a strike that became the Baptist War.</blockquote><a href="/movement/abolitionism">Abolitionism · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
